
Potential Visual language for website 

Overall feel
Clean, friendly, craft-shop minimalism. A mostly black-and-white Shopify layout that steps back and lets colorful pin photography do the talking, with a hand-made touch from the logo and an eco/green accent layered on top.
Logo
•	Hand-lettered script wordmark (“Enamel Pin”) with small, spaced-out sans caps for “THE” and “FACTORY” tucked around it.
•	Black monoline, slightly retro, like sign painting or a vintage badge. It’s the only “personality” typography on the site.
Color palette
Role	Color	Notes
Base text / buttons	Black #000000	Dominant color by far
Background	White #FFFFFF	Lots of white space
Accent (stars, highlights)	Coral #EE6549	Warm pop used for review stars
Footer	Deep navy #141D29	Grounds the bottom of the page
Countdown tiles	Slate navy #242B3E	Flip-clock style timer
Promo banner	Soft leaf green	Ties into the eco/”GreenPins” messaging
Secondary text	Grays #333, #666, #999	Captions, item counts
The real color comes from the product photos: pastel blues, pinks, and mint greens.
Typography
•	DM Sans for everything (body, headings, buttons). A geometric, rounded, friendly sans.
•	Headings are bold (700) with slightly wide letter spacing, centered, often with a short underline rule beneath.
•	Body text is small and light (around 13px), keeping the page airy.
Imagery
•	Lifestyle flat-lays: pins on fabric, denim, and tote bags with soft natural light and shadows (the hero shows pins on a gray sweatshirt).
•	Solid pastel backdrops for product shots: powder blue, blush pink, sage green.
•	Category images are cropped into circles, which echoes the round badge shape. That’s their signature graphic device.
•	A row of client logos (Nintendo, Sanrio, Panasonic, The Body Shop, Fangamer, Walker Books) for trust.
UI elements
•	Solid black buttons with lightly rounded corners (about 5px), white medium-weight text.
•	Flip-clock countdown in the sale banner for urgency.
•	A black vertical “★ Reviews” tab stuck to the right edge.
•	Thin dotted borders separating sections; no heavy shadows or gradients.
Copy tone
Warm, plain, and practical: “Bringing your pin designs to life!”, “Get Creative”, “Let customers speak for us”. Benefit bullets are short and factual (Made in the UK, recycled materials, free artwork setup, fast production).
Homepage structure
Promo banner → logo header → lifestyle hero with CTA → benefit strip → circular category grid → reviews → Eco vs Enamel comparison → client logos → stickers cross-sell → newsletter → navy footer.
Takeaways if you’re building your own pin brand
What works: neutral UI so the pins pop, circular crops, real-life flat-lay photos, and one distinctive logo. Where you could stand out: they lean generic (stock Shopify layout, one font), so a stronger custom accent color or a more distinctive heading font would make your site feel less templated. Your photography skills would also be a real edge here, since product photos carry their whole look.
